  "sources": [
    "J.H. Hutton, The Angami Nagas (1921), pp. 101-102 and Fig. II — 'A variant form is played with nine pieces on each side' with 'triangular refuges'; refuge move as quoted above.",
    "Ludii DLP Evidence 833 'Terhüchü' — 9 pieces each on the expanded board with triangular extensions (cross-check).",
    "Wikipedia 'Terhüchü' — eight triangles x 6 points = 48 added to 25 = 73 points (secondary cross-check of the point count)."
  ],
  "gaps": [
    "Triangle point coordinates are proportioned from Hutton's printed Fig. II (inner cross-lines/arcs at about half extension, outer boundaries about one board-square out); exact drafting positions are approximate, but the connection topology is as drawn.",
    "Corner extensions are drawn as quarter-circle fans rather than straight-sided triangles; encoded as radial/arc intersections as drawn.",
    "Whether Hutton's 'may skip one junction' is optional or required inside refuges is ambiguous in his wording.",
    "Starting player not documented — omitted.",
    "Variant labels 'v1'/'v3' are project nomenclature; Hutton does not number the forms (see terhuchu-v1 gaps)."
  ]
